--- !u!114 &-7715393057401881179
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: 'In this example, let''s override the exposure globally.


      1. Right click in the <i>Hierarchy,</i> and create a <b>Volume</b> > <b>Global Volume.</b>


      2. Create a <b>New</b> Profile using the dedicated button.


      3. In the Volume component''s <i>Inspector,</i> set the <b>Priority</b> to 1000, to ensure this volume can override all the other volumes in the Scene.


      4. Add an <b>Exposure</b> override, set its <b>Mode</b> to <b>Fixed</b> and its <b>Fixed Exposure</b> value to 3.


      5. Head to the other rooms which receive a larger amount of light, and notice how much brighter they appear, almost solid white.


      6. Using the <b>Fixed Exposure</b> slider, increase the Exposure value between 8 and 13 in order to accomodate for these brighter rooms.


      As you can see, the <b>Fixed</b> Exposure Mode requires the exposure to be manually tuned for every lighting scenario. This might not be an acceptable solution for dynamic scenarios displaying a wide range of lighting intensities like the ones found in this Scene, from a relatively dark and artificially lit interior, to a bright outdoor area lit by the sun.'
