--- !u!114 &-4126920598930794041
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "In HDRP, mastering <b>Exposure</b> is important to achieve believable visuals.\r\n\r\nExposure is the amount of lighting affecting the sensor of a camera. Cameras, like your eyes, can automatically adapt to the amount of light in the environment. However, like in real life, it\u2019s important to control the exposure of your HDRP cameras to prevent over- or underexposure.\r\n\r\nThere are multiple exposure modes in HDRP. The most notable are:\r\n\r\n<b>\u2022 Fixed:</b> It allows you to manually set the Scene exposure precisely. As a result, the camera will not try to adapt to the lighting conditions when leaving or entering dark areas. It is used whenever the lighting conditions remain constant.\r\n\r\n<b>\u2022 Automatic Histogram:</b> It simulates how a camera automatically adapts to dynamic lighting conditions within certain limits. This mode is utilized when dealing with a Scene having a wide variety of lighting intensities, such as indoor and outdoor areas."
